1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,790
Edo, winter 1858.

2
00:00:02,870 --> 00:00:04,620
Snow falls on a wooden bridge.

3
00:00:04,750 --> 00:00:06,160
You smell charcoal smoke.

4
00:00:06,240 --> 00:00:07,610
Your breath turns white.

5
00:00:07,740 --> 00:00:09,410
A shop sign catches your eye.

6
00:00:09,490 --> 00:00:10,730
It says mountain whale.

7
00:00:10,860 --> 00:00:11,320
Wait.

8
00:00:11,400 --> 00:00:13,160
There are no whales in the mountains.

9
00:00:13,290 --> 00:00:14,280
It's wild boar.

10
00:00:14,360 --> 00:00:16,350
And that funny name is a cover story.

11
00:00:16,480 --> 00:00:20,635
For more than a thousand years, Japan
had mostly avoided beef and other farm

12
00:00:20,635 --> 00:00:21,050
animals.

13
00:00:21,180 --> 00:00:23,600
The first ban came in 675.

14
00:00:23,680 --> 00:00:25,260
And it had holes from day one.

15
00:00:25,390 --> 00:00:27,750
Yet today, Japan is famous for wagyu.

16
00:00:27,830 --> 00:00:29,680
It's some of the priciest beef on Earth.

17
00:00:29,810 --> 00:00:33,620
So why did Japan avoid beef
for about 1,200 years?

18
00:00:33,740 --> 00:00:35,740
And how did it become the land of wagyu?

19
00:00:35,910 --> 00:00:36,830
Here's the twist.

20
00:00:36,910 --> 00:00:38,650
Japan never fully banned meat.

21
00:00:38,730 --> 00:00:41,910
It spent 1,200 years inventing loopholes.

22
00:00:42,040 --> 00:00:43,310
It isn't one reason.

23
00:00:43,390 --> 00:00:45,230
It's six, all tangled together.

24
00:00:45,360 --> 00:00:48,650
One starts in a muddy rice
field, behind a patient ox.

25
00:00:48,780 --> 00:00:50,670
Another is a polite thank-you letter.

26
00:00:50,750 --> 00:00:51,590
About beef.

27
00:00:51,720 --> 00:00:54,550
And one ends at the palace
gate, with the Emperor himself.

28
00:00:54,680 --> 00:00:56,690
Every reason is a piece of this puzzle.

29
00:00:56,770 --> 00:00:58,660
When all six fit, you'll have the answer.

30
00:00:58,790 --> 00:01:00,410
The first piece is the oldest.

31
00:01:00,490 --> 00:01:02,410
It's a law, and it left the door open.

32
00:01:02,540 --> 00:01:04,730
Japan, the year 675.

33
00:01:04,810 --> 00:01:06,720
Emperor Tenmu sends out a new order.

34
00:01:06,850 --> 00:01:09,610
We can still read it in an
old book, the Nihon Shoki.

35
00:01:09,740 --> 00:01:10,750
The order is short.

36
00:01:10,830 --> 00:01:13,990
Don't eat cows, horses,
dogs, monkeys or chickens.

37
00:01:14,120 --> 00:01:16,740
It also limits some hunting
traps and fish traps.

38
00:01:16,870 --> 00:01:18,030
But look at what's missing.

39
00:01:18,110 --> 00:01:20,200
Deer and wild boar aren't on the list.

40
00:01:20,330 --> 00:01:23,980
And those were the wild meats
people in Japan had eaten for ages.

41
00:01:24,110 --> 00:01:28,270
The ban also seems tied to the farming
months, roughly late spring to autumn.

42
00:01:28,400 --> 00:01:30,950
Historians still argue
whether it ran all year.

43
00:01:31,030 --> 00:01:33,120
The old text can be read both ways.

44
00:01:33,250 --> 00:01:37,150
So the law was strict about cows and
horses, but quiet about deer and boar.

45
00:01:37,280 --> 00:01:39,090
And Tenmu's order wasn't the last.

46
00:01:39,170 --> 00:01:42,190
Later rulers kept giving
similar orders for centuries.

47
00:01:42,320 --> 00:01:44,570
So the law set a rule, with gaps built in.

48
00:01:44,650 --> 00:01:45,960
That's piece one: LAW.

49
00:01:46,090 --> 00:01:49,310
But a law alone can't last 1,200 years.

50
00:01:49,390 --> 00:01:50,940
For that, you need a rice field.

51
00:01:51,070 --> 00:01:53,510
Imagine you're a rice farmer
in the eighth century.

52
00:01:53,590 --> 00:01:55,720
Your whole year depends
on this muddy field.

53
00:01:55,850 --> 00:01:57,930
Plowing that mud by hand is brutal.

54
00:01:58,010 --> 00:02:00,140
So you have help: a big, calm ox.

55
00:02:00,270 --> 00:02:02,970
If that ox could talk,
he'd say: I pull the plow.

56
00:02:03,050 --> 00:02:04,020
I'm not lunch.

57
00:02:04,150 --> 00:02:08,010
In 741, Emperor Shomu repeated
the rule in a new order.

58
00:02:08,090 --> 00:02:09,980
No killing cattle or horses.

59
00:02:10,110 --> 00:02:11,380
His reason was simple.

60
00:02:11,460 --> 00:02:13,380
These animals work in place of people.

61
00:02:13,460 --> 00:02:14,700
Their work feeds people.

62
00:02:14,830 --> 00:02:16,200
So a cow wasn't dinner.

63
00:02:16,280 --> 00:02:17,280
It was your tractor.

64
00:02:17,410 --> 00:02:18,810
And there's a second rice link.

65
00:02:18,890 --> 00:02:22,450
Some people believed meat during
rice season could ruin the harvest.

66
00:02:22,580 --> 00:02:25,520
That may be why the old ban
lined up with the growing months.

67
00:02:25,650 --> 00:02:27,610
And the strangest part is still ahead.

68
00:02:27,690 --> 00:02:30,030
This rice logic lasted for centuries.

69
00:02:30,160 --> 00:02:32,840
Jump to 1587.

70
00:02:32,920 --> 00:02:35,990
The powerful ruler Toyotomi
Hideyoshi writes a memo.

71
00:02:36,120 --> 00:02:37,620
Item eleven is blunt.

72
00:02:37,700 --> 00:02:41,110
Buying, selling, killing or eating
cattle and horses is wrong.

73
00:02:41,240 --> 00:02:44,960
More than nine hundred years after
Tenmu, the cow was still a worker first.

74
00:02:45,090 --> 00:02:46,520
That's piece two: RICE.

75
00:02:46,600 --> 00:02:48,470
The cow was simply too useful to eat.

76
00:02:48,600 --> 00:02:50,750
But usefulness doesn't explain one thing.

77
00:02:50,830 --> 00:02:52,560
Why did meat start to feel wrong?

78
00:02:52,730 --> 00:02:53,700
Picture a temple.

79
00:02:53,780 --> 00:02:56,010
Incense drifts past a wooden pagoda.

80
00:02:56,140 --> 00:02:58,960
Emperor Tenmu was a strong
supporter of Buddhism.

81
00:02:59,040 --> 00:03:01,820
And Buddhism asks people
not to kill living things.

82
00:03:01,950 --> 00:03:05,530
Historians often link his order
to the Buddhist idea of rebirth.

83
00:03:05,610 --> 00:03:07,420
Lives come back again and again.

84
00:03:07,550 --> 00:03:08,790
But there's a second thread.

85
00:03:08,870 --> 00:03:11,530
It comes from Shinto,
Japan's old local faith.

86
00:03:11,660 --> 00:03:13,640
Shinto has an idea called kegare.

87
00:03:13,720 --> 00:03:15,430
Basically, ritual impurity.

88
00:03:15,560 --> 00:03:16,900
It's not about being dirty.

89
00:03:16,980 --> 00:03:20,160
Think of it as a state that keeps
you away from sacred rituals.

90
00:03:20,290 --> 00:03:24,720
In 927, a rulebook called the
Engishiki spelled out the details.

91
00:03:24,850 --> 00:03:28,360
Eat meat, and you had to stay away
from shrine rituals for three days.

92
00:03:28,490 --> 00:03:29,270
Three days.

93
00:03:29,350 --> 00:03:31,630
That's a long time-out
for one bowl of stew.

94
00:03:31,760 --> 00:03:32,810
And that feeling grew.

95
00:03:32,890 --> 00:03:36,090
Over the centuries, meat slowly
drifted off people's plates.

96
00:03:36,220 --> 00:03:40,568
Sadly, the families who did butchering
and leather work, in buraku communities,

97
00:03:40,568 --> 00:03:42,620
faced unfair treatment for centuries.

98
00:03:42,750 --> 00:03:46,040
So faith and ritual turned an
old rule into a deep habit.

99
00:03:46,170 --> 00:03:47,710
That's piece three: PURITY.

100
00:03:47,790 --> 00:03:50,560
We're halfway there, and the
weirdest pieces are still coming.

101
00:03:50,690 --> 00:03:53,190
But if people avoided
meat, what kept them going?

102
00:03:53,310 --> 00:03:54,880
The answer was all around them.

103
00:03:55,010 --> 00:03:56,790
Japan is a chain of islands.

104
00:03:56,870 --> 00:03:58,360
The sea is never far away.

105
00:03:58,490 --> 00:04:00,650
And the meat bans mostly left fish alone.

106
00:04:00,730 --> 00:04:02,450
Fish and rice stayed on the table.

107
00:04:02,580 --> 00:04:04,230
So people still had protein.

108
00:04:04,310 --> 00:04:06,270
That made the ban much
easier to live with.

109
00:04:06,400 --> 00:04:08,200
Here's how deep that habit runs.

110
00:04:08,280 --> 00:04:12,160
In Japanese homes, meat only
passed fresh fish in 2007.

111
00:04:12,290 --> 00:04:13,890
Not 1907.

112
00:04:13,950 --> 00:04:15,100
2007.

113
00:04:15,180 --> 00:04:16,660
That's very recent history.

114
00:04:16,790 --> 00:04:19,750
Fish eating has dropped
since its peak in 2001.

115
00:04:19,830 --> 00:04:22,200
Back then, it was about 40 kilos a person.

116
00:04:22,330 --> 00:04:23,020
Quick guess.

117
00:04:23,080 --> 00:04:26,180
How much beef does the average
person in Japan eat each year?

118
00:04:26,300 --> 00:04:28,850
One kilo, twenty-five kilos, or six?

119
00:04:28,950 --> 00:04:29,740
Take a guess.

120
00:04:29,820 --> 00:04:31,050
About six kilos.

121
00:04:31,110 --> 00:04:32,940
That's roughly half a kilo a month.

122
00:04:33,070 --> 00:04:35,080
So beef is still a
small part of the plate.

123
00:04:35,160 --> 00:04:36,670
Fish keeps a much bigger share.

124
00:04:36,800 --> 00:04:37,740
One quick thing.

125
00:04:37,820 --> 00:04:40,360
Next week: why Europe
was afraid of the potato.

126
00:04:40,440 --> 00:04:42,010
Subscribe so it finds you.

127
00:04:42,140 --> 00:04:43,690
The sea made the ban easy to keep.

128
00:04:43,770 --> 00:04:45,190
That's piece four: SEA.

129
00:04:45,320 --> 00:04:47,270
But easy isn't the same as happy.

130
00:04:47,350 --> 00:04:49,100
Some people still wanted meat.

131
00:04:49,180 --> 00:04:50,260
So they got creative.

132
00:04:50,390 --> 00:04:52,330
Back to that snowy street in Edo.

133
00:04:52,410 --> 00:04:53,720
Remember the mountain whale?

134
00:04:53,890 --> 00:04:58,070
This is a real print by
Hiroshige, from 1858.

135
00:04:58,150 --> 00:04:59,620
Look at the left for that sign.

136
00:04:59,750 --> 00:05:01,760
Shops like this had a clever excuse.

137
00:05:01,840 --> 00:05:04,590
They called it kusurigui,
which means medicine eating.

138
00:05:04,720 --> 00:05:06,180
You weren't eating meat for fun.

139
00:05:06,260 --> 00:05:07,640
No, this was for your health.

140
00:05:07,770 --> 00:05:09,900
Wild boar even got a pretty nickname.

141
00:05:09,980 --> 00:05:12,030
Botan, which means peony, the flower.

142
00:05:12,160 --> 00:05:14,650
If the boar could talk,
he'd say: I'm a whale now.

143
00:05:14,730 --> 00:05:15,650
Please don't ask.

144
00:05:15,780 --> 00:05:19,320
By the late Edo period, shops
selling wild meat were doing well.

145
00:05:19,450 --> 00:05:21,330
But the boldest loophole wasn't boar.

146
00:05:21,410 --> 00:05:22,030
It was beef.

147
00:05:22,160 --> 00:05:25,420
Meet Hikone, a castle town in
what's now Shiga Prefecture.

148
00:05:25,550 --> 00:05:30,570
The story goes that in 1687, a man
here made beef pickled in miso.

149
00:05:30,700 --> 00:05:32,790
He based it on a Chinese medicine book.

150
00:05:32,870 --> 00:05:35,230
And he gave it a medicine name: henpongan.

151
00:05:35,360 --> 00:05:41,363
In the 1780s, Hikone sent miso beef to
the family of the shogun, Japan's military

152
00:05:41,363 --> 00:05:41,810
ruler.

153
00:05:41,940 --> 00:05:45,400
Hikone is often called the only
place allowed to make beef back then.

154
00:05:45,530 --> 00:05:47,140
And the next part sounds made up.

155
00:05:47,220 --> 00:05:47,900
It isn't.

156
00:05:48,030 --> 00:05:53,160
Imagine you're Tokugawa Nariaki,
a powerful lord, in 1848.

157
00:05:53,240 --> 00:05:55,450
Another gift of beef arrives from Hikone.

158
00:05:55,580 --> 00:05:57,860
So you write a thank-you
note to Hikone's lord.

159
00:05:57,990 --> 00:05:59,870
Thank you for the beef you keep sending.

160
00:05:59,950 --> 00:06:01,560
I use it as medicine too.

161
00:06:01,690 --> 00:06:05,220
So even top lords ate beef, as
long as it was called medicine.

162
00:06:05,350 --> 00:06:06,820
Meat never really vanished.

163
00:06:06,900 --> 00:06:08,340
It just changed its name.

164
00:06:08,420 --> 00:06:10,150
That's piece five: LOOPHOLES.

165
00:06:10,280 --> 00:06:12,630
But loopholes only work
while the rule stands.

166
00:06:12,710 --> 00:06:14,240
And the rule was about to fall.

167
00:06:14,370 --> 00:06:17,020
Look at this tiny island
in Nagasaki harbor.

168
00:06:17,100 --> 00:06:18,300
It's called Dejima.

169
00:06:18,430 --> 00:06:19,800
Dutch traders lived here.

170
00:06:19,880 --> 00:06:22,050
And they kept cows, pigs and chickens.

171
00:06:22,180 --> 00:06:27,500
This print from 1861 shows foreigners
from five nations at a banquet.

172
00:06:27,580 --> 00:06:28,840
The meat-eaters had arrived.

173
00:06:28,970 --> 00:06:33,230
And in 1862, Yokohama got
its first gyunabe shop.

174
00:06:33,310 --> 00:06:34,850
That's beef simmered in a pot.

175
00:06:34,980 --> 00:06:37,730
Then came early 1872.

176
00:06:37,810 --> 00:06:40,180
Emperor Meiji lifted the old ban on meat.

177
00:06:40,310 --> 00:06:42,870
And the court let it be known
that he would eat meat himself.

178
00:06:43,000 --> 00:06:44,600
His reason wasn't mainly health.

179
00:06:44,680 --> 00:06:48,000
He told an advisor meat mattered
more for dealing with foreigners.

180
00:06:48,130 --> 00:06:49,390
Not everyone agreed.

181
00:06:49,470 --> 00:06:53,539
About a month later, ten mountain ascetics
in white tried to force their way into

182
00:06:53,539 --> 00:06:54,090
the palace.

183
00:06:54,220 --> 00:06:56,900
Ascetics are people who give
up comforts for their faith.

184
00:06:56,980 --> 00:06:59,780
These men followed a faith
centered on Mount Ontake.

185
00:06:59,910 --> 00:07:02,630
They believed foreign ways
were making the land impure.

186
00:07:02,710 --> 00:07:04,940
The guards stopped them,
and four of the men died.

187
00:07:05,070 --> 00:07:07,240
For them, this was about faith, not food.

188
00:07:07,370 --> 00:07:09,280
But in the cities, beef caught on fast.

189
00:07:09,360 --> 00:07:11,870
People sat cross-legged
around steaming pots.

190
00:07:12,000 --> 00:07:14,540
Kanagaki Robun even wrote a book about it.

191
00:07:14,620 --> 00:07:16,850
Its name means cross-legged
at the beef pot.

192
00:07:16,980 --> 00:07:17,650
Your turn.

193
00:07:17,710 --> 00:07:22,320
By 1877, how many beef pot
shops were there in Tokyo?

194
00:07:22,440 --> 00:07:25,970
About 50, over 550, or about 5,000?

195
00:07:26,070 --> 00:07:26,860
Take a guess.

196
00:07:26,940 --> 00:07:28,480
Over 550.

197
00:07:28,540 --> 00:07:30,850
And that's just five
years after the ban ended.

198
00:07:30,980 --> 00:07:34,050
Now look at Tokyo's cattle
for beef over thirty years.

199
00:07:34,130 --> 00:07:35,990
The number grew more than seven times.

200
00:07:36,120 --> 00:07:39,510
With the emperor on board, city
menus changed within decades.

201
00:07:39,640 --> 00:07:41,200
That's piece six: EMPEROR.

202
00:07:41,280 --> 00:07:42,700
And the puzzle is complete.

203
00:07:42,830 --> 00:07:44,920
So, remember that shop sign in the snow?

204
00:07:45,040 --> 00:07:46,580
That funny name was a loophole.

205
00:07:46,710 --> 00:07:47,830
Here's the whole answer.

206
00:07:47,910 --> 00:07:51,740
Law, rice and purity rules pushed beef
away, and the sea made that easy.

207
00:07:51,870 --> 00:07:54,920
Loopholes kept meat alive,
until an emperor ended the ban.

208
00:07:55,250 --> 00:07:56,670
And here's the last twist.

209
00:07:56,750 --> 00:08:00,360
Kobe beef, a famous kind of
wagyu, comes from Tajima cattle.

210
00:08:00,490 --> 00:08:02,580
For a long time, those
cattle weren't food.

211
00:08:02,660 --> 00:08:04,720
They were work animals that pulled plows.

212
00:08:04,850 --> 00:08:09,740
Then Kobe's port opened in 1868,
and foreigners started eating them.

213
00:08:09,870 --> 00:08:13,180
Today, Kobe beef is some of the
most expensive beef in the world.

214
00:08:13,260 --> 00:08:14,470
From plow to plate.

215
00:08:14,600 --> 00:08:16,050
Still, the sea never lost.

216
00:08:16,130 --> 00:08:19,070
People in Japan eat about
six kilos of beef a year.

217
00:08:19,200 --> 00:08:21,070
And about 21 kilos of fish.

218
00:08:21,150 --> 00:08:24,450
In the land of wagyu, fish still
wins, more than three to one.

219
00:08:24,580 --> 00:08:27,870
So that's how a country that
avoided beef ended up famous for it.

220
00:08:28,000 --> 00:08:30,920
Next week, a whole continent
gets scared of a vegetable.

221
00:08:31,000 --> 00:08:33,000
Why was Europe afraid of the potato?

222
00:08:33,170 --> 00:08:34,740
Subscribe so it finds you.

223
00:08:34,820 --> 00:08:37,220
Until then, tell me one
thing in the comments.

224
00:08:37,350 --> 00:08:39,820
What food would you call medicine,
just so you could eat it?

225
00:08:39,940 --> 00:08:40,910
See you next week.
